			},
			"date": "2025-09-10T17:46:52.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> yes, this is language dependant. Each browser has hyphenation dictionaries for various languages. In German, you’ll notice that there is hyphenation for capitalized words. This would actually be a very good Interop issue. </p><p>I’ve got some demos I made in the past for this…<br>With English: <a href=\"https://knowler.dev/demos/tp0-gpS?codepen\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">knowler.dev/demos/tp0-gpS?code</span><span class=\"invisible\">pen</span></a><br>With a German word: <a href=\"https://knowler.dev/demos/idITprB?codepen\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">knowler.dev/demos/idITprB?code</span><span class=\"invisible\">pen</span></a></p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "115181260872507477",
			"parentId": "115181250985987400",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/115181260872507477",
			"author": {
